import{_ as n,o as i,c as a,a3 as o}from"./chunks/framework.BwzYn71E.js";const h=JSON.parse('{"title":"Video Script","description":"","frontmatter":{"translationSource":{"title":"视频文案","archivedAt":"2026-11-08","url":"https://docs.qq.com/aio/DTmluZ1diWlpQZldw?p=B3tojB4zGjZ30BLLdL9qvK","scope":"page"},"translationLang":"en","head":[["link",{"rel":"canonical","href":"https://kokobird28144.github.io/endfield-skydoc/all-condition-xircon-video-script"}],["link",{"rel":"alternate","hreflang":"en","href":"https://kokobird28144.github.io/endfield-skydoc/all-condition-xircon-video-script"}],["link",{"rel":"alternate","hreflang":"zh-CN","href":"https://kokobird28144.github.io/endfield-skydoc/zh/all-condition-xircon-video-script"}],["link",{"rel":"alternate","hreflang":"x-default","href":"https://kokobird28144.github.io/endfield-skydoc/all-condition-xircon-video-script"}]]},"headers":[],"relativePath":"all-condition-xircon-video-script.md","filePath":"logistics/src/page-B3tojB4zGjZ30BLLdL9qvK.md"}'),t={name:"all-condition-xircon-video-script.md"};function r(s,e,l,c,d,p){return i(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[o('<div class="logistics-content"><h1 id="video-script" tabindex="-1">Video Script <a class="header-anchor" href="#video-script" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Video Script&quot;">​</a></h1><blockquote><p>Source page ID: <code>B3tojB4zGjZ30BLLdL9qvK</code> | <a href="https://docs.qq.com/aio/DTmluZ1diWlpQZldw?p=B3tojB4zGjZ30BLLdL9qvK" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Open in Tencent Docs</a></p></blockquote><p><span id="source-block-RDbBJi6vkeN255vNGHDFry"></span></p><h2 id="introduction" tabindex="-1">Introduction <a class="header-anchor" href="#introduction" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Introduction&quot;">​</a></h2><p>In our adaptive maximum-gross-production blueprints and many others, the Xircon module has a problem: prolonged lack of Xiranite, or insufficient Xiranite supply, can easily produce a self-locking state that does not clear automatically. We can avoid insufficient Xiranite in several ways, such as the new full-storage oscillation plan we are working on. But our research has now produced this all-condition Xircon setup, intended to solve every possible problem in one go.</p><p><span class="mark-grey">#言, script<br> Since the Xircon module first appeared in 1.2, most versions people see have gained resistance to full Xircon storage. But insufficient inputs (show Clean Water, Sewage, Ferrium Powder and Xiranite on screen) can still cause “self-locking,” permanently reducing the reaction rate. Admittedly, input shortages rarely happen under normal operating conditions, so we could just teach everyone how to fix it—switching automatic cleanup on and off is hardly much trouble.<br> But that&#39;s how weaklings think! (Insert that well-known image.)<br> If our Xircon module can withstand every adverse operating condition, the problem simply disappears, also ending the era in which SC battery production was less reliable than LC battery production. So we designed this “all-condition Xircon line.”</span></p><p><span id="source-block-B2oIxTvH7Xxbz4jsN9yLX2"></span></p><h2 id="features" tabindex="-1">Features <a class="header-anchor" href="#features" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Features&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Under all external disturbances (<span class="mark-red">with/without Xircon Effluent from the Purification Node</span>, <span class="mark-orange">with/without full Xircon storage</span>, <span class="mark-yellow">only Xiranite missing / only Clean Water missing / only Sewage missing / only Ferrium Powder missing / Xiranite and Clean Water missing / Xiranite and Sewage missing / Xiranite and Ferrium Powder missing / Clean Water and Sewage missing / Xiranite and Sewage and missing / Clean Water and Sewage missing / Xiranite, Clean Water and Sewage missing / Xiranite, Clean Water and Ferrium Powder missing / Xiranite, Sewage and Ferrium Powder missing / Clean Water, Sewage and Ferrium Powder missing / only Xiranite available / only Clean Water available / only Sewage available / only Ferrium Powder available</span>), it does not self-lock or waste inputs. It converges automatically to maximum output for the given inputs, does not slow down, and resumes normal production automatically after inputs are sufficiently restored or full Xircon storage is cleared.</p><p>Power-saving (3 expanded reaction tanks and 2 reaction tanks, 400 power in total, with simultaneous medicinal-fluid production supported).</p><p><span class="mark-grey">#言, comment<br> The wording seems a bit awkward.</span></p><p><span id="source-block-ImY1wA0pyXFZ4wWMXGiE6D"></span></p><h2 id="principles" tabindex="-1">Principles <a class="header-anchor" href="#principles" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Principles&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Self-locking mechanism: self-locking occurs when <span class="mark-green">Xircon Effluent</span> and <span class="mark-purple">Sewage</span> both reach inventories of 50 while both the Xircon Effluent reaction (Liquid Xiranite + Sewage → Xircon Effluent + Inert Xircon Effluent) and the Xircon reaction (Xircon Effluent + Ferrium Powder → Xircon + Sewage) are in progress.</p><p>Self-locking under insufficient Xiranite: with insufficient Xiranite and continuous Xircon Effluent input, conversion of Xircon Effluent produces Sewage until both inventories reach 50. No Xircon Effluent reaction is active, so there is no self-locking yet. Supplying Xiranite consumes 1 Sewage to start the Xircon Effluent reaction; completion of a Xircon reaction produces 1 Sewage, restoring Sewage to 50, then 2 Xircon Effluent are consumed to start another Xircon reaction. Inputting 2 Xircon Effluent over 1s restores Xircon Effluent to 50. Both inventories are now 50, both reactions are active, and self-locking occurs. By contrast, inputting <span class="mark-purple">Sewage</span> does not self-lock.</p><p>Self-locking with full Xircon storage: once Xircon is blocked, its reaction is blocked completely. With continuous Sewage input, the tank producing Xircon still performs the Xircon Effluent reaction normally, so both inventories reach 50 and naturally enter self-locking. By contrast, inputting <span class="mark-green">Xircon Effluent</span> does not self-lock.</p><p>Automatic unlocking: letting <span class="mark-green">Xircon Effluent</span> or <span class="mark-purple">Sewage</span> flow out automatically clears self-locking. If output events are too infrequent, however, locking and unlocking repeat and prevent the maximum rate from being reached.</p><p>Principle of the all-condition Xircon line: keep Sewage flowing so that it can never become completely backed up. The expanded reaction tank therefore cannot self-lock through both Xircon Effluent and Sewage filling simultaneously. In other words, any self-locking state can always be cleared, so self-locking does not occur. Meanwhile, Sewage flow provides enough free space for the Xircon reaction.</p><p><span class="mark-grey">#言, comment<br> Isn&#39;t this too long-winded?</span></p><p><span class="mark-grey">#言, script<br> Self-locking begins → self-locking clears<br> Synchronizing Sewage output with Xircon production — whether it can reach full rate / return to full rate after slowing down.</span></p><p><span id="source-block-G4ZqSXlTAuffakno2II1eB"></span></p><h2 id="usage-tutorial" tabindex="-1">Usage Tutorial <a class="header-anchor" href="#usage-tutorial" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Usage Tutorial&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Connect through conduits: 60 Clean Water (oversupply allowed), 60 Sewage (oversupply allowed; preferably decoupled from Cuprium—ignore that if it makes no sense), 30 Liquid Xiranite (oversupply allowed), and 0/12 Xircon Effluent (oversupply allowed).</p><p><span class="mark-grey">#言, comment<br> Who is going to understand “Cuprium decoupling”? I think we should leave it out. A later video will properly explain “why the final product need not be destroyed.” If you insist on mentioning it, provide the decoupling module too. Talking about Sewage decoupling without saying exactly what the module destroys is too vague.</span></p><p>90 Xiranite and 90 Ferrium Powder are already connected.</p><p>Normal output: 75 Xircon (without the Purification Node) / 81 (with the Purification Node).</p><p><span id="source-block-gAZAiMzy7xC6vLFMe3NjDK"></span></p><h2 id="is-this-the-end" tabindex="-1">Is This the End? <a class="header-anchor" href="#is-this-the-end" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Is This the End?&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Of course not. Several imperfections immediately come to mind: internal Liquid Xiranite cannot be output when Xircon storage is full, and reaction-tank utilization is not maximized during normal operation (show the small Xircon tank?).</p><p><span id="source-block-yJdofzx1Eu4IOVqZbl05PN"></span></p><h2 id="closing" tabindex="-1">Closing <a class="header-anchor" href="#closing" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Closing&quot;">​</a></h2><p>This is the first time we have picked out an individual AIC module for a video to share our latest development results. Please leave comments and suggestions, and like, favorite and give a coin if you can. Thanks, everyone!</p><p><span class="mark-grey">#言, comment<br> Is it the first time? Didn&#39;t 百亿 make a few explanations of automatic-control setups? (</span></p><p><span class="mark-grey">The first on 斯改&#39;s account ((</span></p></div>',1)])])}const f=n(t,[["render",r]]);export{h as __pageData,f as default};
